  "intro": "# Grunfeld setups\n\nBlack fianchettos with ...g6 and also puts a pawn on d5. It looks like the last chapter and plays quite differently, so check the d-pawn before you choose a plan.\n\n## The one difference that matters\n\nIn the King's Indian setups Black plays ...d6 and White answers with c4, taking a big centre. Here Black has a pawn on d5, so c4 achieves nothing and costs something: it opens lines for the bishop on g7, which is the piece you want to keep looking at your solid pawn on d4.\n\nSo the pawn goes to c3 and stays there. This is the slow chapter.\n\n## Your setup\n\nBf4, e3, Nf3, Be2, castle, c3, h3, Nbd2, then Ne5 and f4. The bishop goes to e2 for the same reason as the last chapter: the pawn on g6 covers h7, so d3 aims at a defended square.\n\nThe h3 move is worth playing early. Black's ...Nh5 is the standard nuisance in these positions, and Bh2 is a much better answer than Bg3.\n\n## Where the play comes from\n\nWhite's chances are the e5 square and the kingside. Black's are the long diagonal and the queenside. Keep the centre closed and you keep the better half of that bargain: the bishop on g7 spends the game staring at a pawn on d4 that has two defenders.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nPlaying c4 out of habit from the King's Indian chapter and opening the diagonal. Taking on c5, which does the same thing for free. Putting the bishop on d3 where the g6 pawn makes it pointless. Jumping to e5 before c3, d4 and the knight on d2 are there to support it.",
